# Line endings are LF, everywhere, on every platform.
#
# This repository is developed on Linux, macOS and Windows, and every text file
# in it is LF. Without this file git has no opinion on the matter: it compares
# bytes, so any Windows tool that rewrites a file in place -- npm linking this
# package into an app, an editor saving with its own default -- produces a
# whole-file diff that is a line ending and nothing else. That is not
# hypothetical. It blocked a pull, and `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` returning
# an empty diff was the only way to tell the difference between "nothing
# changed" and "everything changed".
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text and store it LF-normalised in the index;
# `eol=lf` checks it back out as LF rather than the platform's default. The
# scripts are the case that fails loudly rather than silently: a CRLF shebang
# asks the kernel for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Detected as binary already, named here so no future heuristic gets a vote.
*.png binary
